<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px 0; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Polite Request</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Sorry, I Can't Help You.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write instructions for simple tasks. Interactive: Write a simple collaborative text with peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple, clear instructions for a game in a polite manner (e.g., writing, "Please wait for your turn. Then roll the dice."). Can co-write short dialogues with peers using polite requests (e.g., writing, "I have a pencil, but I don't have an eraser. Can I borrow your eraser?" "Good idea. Your turn now."). Can write a short note to explain their inability to complete a task using polite expressions (e.g., writing, "I'm sorry. I can't roll the dice. Can you help me?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards of classroom objects (pencil, paper, desk, bag) and actions (roll, ask, help, wait). Students work in pairs to quickly write down two sentences using these words. For example, "I need a pencil." or "Please help." The teacher collects a few examples to check prior knowledge.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple request on the board: "Can I borrow your pencil, please?" and a refusal: "I'm sorry, I can't. I need it." The teacher reads them aloud, clearly modeling the pronunciation of /p/ in "please" and /k/ in "can't," asking students to repeat. The teacher points to the written sentences, explaining the politeness of "please" and "sorry."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'Can I have your paper?', is it polite?" (Expected answer: Yes, with 'please' it's very polite.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I can't help you.', am I saying 'yes' or 'no'?" (Expected answer: No.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, written collaborative dialogue between two friends and a set of simple game instructions. Both texts use the target vocabulary (pencil, eraser, friend, turn, roll, dice, please, sorry, can, can't) and grammar (polite requests, present simple, negative contracted form of 'can').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><u>Example Dialogue:</u><br>Friend 1: I have a pencil, but I don't have an eraser. Can I borrow your eraser, please?<br>Friend 2: I'm sorry. I can't. I need it for my turn. Can you ask another friend?<br>Friend 1: Okay. I will ask my friend over there. Thank you!</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><u>Example Instructions:</u><br>Game: "Friendly Dice"<br>1. Please wait for your turn.<br>2. Roll the dice.<br>3. Move your friendly hand.<br>4. Help your friend if they need it.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Writing coherent sentences and short texts to convey simple messages and instructions in a polite manner.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the dialogue and game instructions silently.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are the friends talking about?" (Expected answer: Borrowing an eraser.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (written response):</b> Students write "T" or "F" next to statements related to the texts.
                            <br>a. Friend 1 has an eraser. (F)
                            <br>b. Friend 2 can borrow the eraser. (F)
                            <br>c. The game asks you to roll a dice. (T)
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on writing polite requests ("Can I have...please?"), expressions of inability ("I can't..."), and simple statements using the present simple ("I have... but I don't have..."). Incorporating vocabulary: pencil, eraser, paper, friend, help, roll, wait, answer.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks, and students choose from a word bank (e.g., please, can't, borrow, help, paper) to complete them.
                            <br>a. Can I ______ your pencil, ______?
                            <br>b. I'm sorry, I ______ help you.
                            <br>c. I have a pen, but I don't have ______.
                            <br>d. Please wait for your ______.
                            <br>Students write the answers individually on a worksheet.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Building with Prompts):</b> Students are given picture prompts (e.g., a hand asking for a pencil, a friend looking sad because they can't lift a heavy bag) and sentence starters. They write complete sentences.
                            <br>a. (Picture of needing a pencil) "I need a pencil. Can I ________?" (Student writes: "Can I borrow your pencil, please?")
                            <br>b. (Picture of a closed book) "I don't know the answer. I ________." (Student writes: "I can't answer.")
                            <br>c. (Picture of a friend holding an empty bag) "I have my books, but I don't have my ______." (Student writes: "I don't have my bag.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Sentence Writing (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives them scenarios (e.g., "You want to play a game, but your friend doesn't have dice," or "You need to write, but you don't have paper"). Students collaboratively write a polite request and a polite refusal/explanation for each scenario using their practice sentences. The teacher circulates, offering assistance and correcting spelling/grammar for accuracy.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Written Product.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of three. Each group will complete two writing tasks:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task 1: Write Simple Game Instructions.</b> Students choose a simple classroom game (e.g., "Roll the Dice and Share") and collaboratively write 3-4 clear, polite instructions for it. They must include "Please," "wait for your turn," and "roll the dice."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task 2: Co-write a Short Note.</b> The teacher provides a scenario: "Your friend needs to borrow your new pencil, but you promised your mom you wouldn't lend it." Students collaboratively write a short note (2-3 sentences) to their friend, politely explaining why they can't lend the pencil. They must use "I'm sorry," "I can't," and "friend" or "kind."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher encourages students to use the vocabulary and grammar from the lesson to ensure their writing is polite and clear. Students can also be encouraged to read their written work aloud to each other, paying attention to the /p/ and /k/ sounds, further developing their phonemic awareness through speech, before finalizing the written product.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their collaboratively written game instructions and polite note from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher checklist):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity: Instructions and note are easy to understand.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Politeness: Uses "please," "thank you," "sorry," and "can't" appropriately.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Target Vocabulary: Incorporates at least 3-4 specified vocabulary words (e.g., pencil, friend, turn, roll, help).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Target Grammar: Correctly uses "Can I...please?", "I can't...", and "I have... but I don't have...".</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration: All group members contributed to the written product.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new polite sentence you wrote today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is it easy or difficult to write polite sentences?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing today's instructions or note was the most fun?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What still feels a little difficult when writing in English?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this writing lesson? Students responded well to collaborative writing and the guided sentence building. The combination of game instructions and personal notes helped cover different writing forms. What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still struggle with correct word order in requests. More focus on sentence structure for polite requests and explanations of inability (subject-verb agreement for "I have"/"I don't have") will be beneficial. I will plan for quick whiteboard sentence drills in the next warm-up.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will involve students summarizing information about polite requests and explanations, possibly translating simple requests, and creating a short class poster or role-play script based on the theme, integrating the written forms practiced today into a final project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>